INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('c8a0602d','general','Crystal Compiler: Pattern for Adding New Language Constructs',replace('Production-ready 5-step process for extending Crystal compiler with new syntax (Phase 31 example - Module/Include/Extend):\n\n**VERIFIED PATTERN (385 tests pass):**\n\n1. **LEXER** (token.cr + lexer.cr):\n   - Add keyword enum: `Module`, `Include`, `Extend`\n   - Add keyword mapping: `when "module" then Token::Kind::Module`\n\n2. **AST** (ast.cr):\n   - Add node kinds: `Module`, `Include`, `Extend`\n   - Add fields: `module_name`, `module_body`, `include_name`, `extend_name`\n   - Add initializer parameters for new fields\n\n3. **PARSER** (parser.cr):\n   - Implement parse methods: `parse_module`, `parse_include`, `parse_extend`\n   - Update integration: `definition_start?` for definitions, `parse_statement` for statements\n   - Module pattern: parse name → parse body → expect ''end''\n   - Include/Extend pattern: parse name → return node\n\n4. **TYPE INFERENCE** (type_inference_engine.cr):\n   - Add cases to `infer_expression`: `.module?`, `.include?`, `.extend?`\n   - Implement infer methods: `infer_module`, `infer_include`, `infer_extend`\n   - Process body for definitions, return nil_type\n\n5. **TESTS** (spec/):\n   - Comprehensive spec file (9+ tests)\n   - Production test file (/tmp/)\n   - Verify: `crystal spec` + `./bin/crystal-gpt5 test_file.cr`\n\n**KEY INSIGHTS:**\n- Module similar to Class (body + end)\n- Include/Extend are statements (just identifier)\n- Must do steps IN ORDER (lexer→AST→parser→inference→tests)\n- Integration points critical: `definition_start?` or `parse_statement`\n\n**EVIDENCE:**\n- Phase 31: 385 tests pass (376 + 9 new)\n- Files: ast.cr (211-213, 275-278, 339-342), parser.cr (1711-1820), type_inference_engine.cr (158-166, 301-335)\n- Production code parses correctly\n\n**APPLICABILITY:** Any new Crystal language construct (keywords, control flow, macros, declarations)\n\n**ANTI-PATTERNS:**\n- ❌ Skipping any step → incomplete implementation\n- ❌ Not testing production code after unit tests\n- ❌ Forgetting integration points → parser never calls new methods\n- ❌ Not adding initializer parameters → compilation error','\n',char(10)),replace('Production-ready 5-step process for extending Crystal compiler with new syntax (Phase 31 example - Module/Include/Extend):\n\n**VERIFIED PATTERN (385 tests pass):**\n\n1. **LEXER** (token.cr + lexer.cr):\n   - Add keyword enum: `Module`, `Include`, `Extend`\n   - Add keyword mapping: `when "module" then Token::Kind::Module`\n\n2. **AST** (ast.cr):\n   - Add node kinds: `Module`, `Include`, `Extend`\n   - Ad...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T08:58:04.805324','2025-10-25T08:58:04.805329',NULL,22,'2025-10-27T20:05:44.044089');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('64b43022','general','Crystal Compiler: Struct Implementation (Code Reuse Pattern)',replace('**Pattern:** Implementing struct by reusing class infrastructure (Phase 32)\n\n**Context:** When adding a new language construct that is syntactically identical to an existing one but semantically different.\n\n## Verified Approach (393 tests pass):\n\n**Problem:** Add struct keyword to Crystal compiler\n- Struct syntax identical to class: `struct Name < Parent ... end`\n- Semantic difference: value type vs reference type\n- Don''t want to duplicate 60+ lines of class parsing logic\n\n**Solution:** Code reuse via boolean flag\n\n### Implementation:\n\n1. **LEXER** (2 lines changed):\n   - Add Struct keyword token\n   - Add keyword mapping\n\n2. **AST** (3 lines changed):\n   - Add Struct node kind\n   - Add `class_is_struct : Bool?` field\n   - Add initializer parameter\n\n3. **PARSER** (12 lines changed):\n   ```crystal\n   # Modify existing method to accept parameter\n   private def parse_class(is_struct : Bool = false) : ExprId\n     # ... existing logic ...\n     kind = is_struct ? ExpressionNode::Kind::Struct : ExpressionNode::Kind::Class\n     # ... create node with kind and class_is_struct: is_struct ...\n   end\n   \n   # Create wrapper method\n   private def parse_struct : ExprId\n     parse_class(is_struct: true)\n   end\n   ```\n   - Update definition_start? to include Struct\n   - Add Struct cases (2 locations)\n\n4. **TYPE INFERENCE** (4 lines changed):\n   ```crystal\n   when .struct?\n     infer_class(node, expr_id)  # Reuse class logic\n   ```\n\n5. **TESTS** (8 comprehensive tests)\n\n### Key Insights:\n\n✅ **Code Reuse > Duplication**\n- 12 lines changed vs 60+ lines duplicated\n- Single source of truth for class/struct parsing\n- Bug fixes apply to both automatically\n\n✅ **Boolean Flag Pattern**\n- `is_struct` parameter with default false\n- Backwards compatible (existing calls work)\n- Clear semantic distinction in AST\n\n✅ **Two-Level Distinction**\n- Node Kind (Struct vs Class) for pattern matching\n- Boolean field (class_is_struct) for semantic queries\n- Both approaches valid, choose based on context\n\n### Evidence:\n- Phase 32: 393 tests pass (385 + 8 new)\n- Files: ast.cr (+3 lines), parser.cr (+12 lines), type_inference_engine.cr (+4 lines)\n- Production code parses correctly\n- Commit: fc048e508\n\n### Applicability:\nUse when adding language construct with:\n- Identical syntax to existing construct\n- Different semantics\n- Example: abstract class, sealed class, record vs struct, etc.\n\n### Anti-Patterns:\n❌ Duplicating entire parse method\n❌ Using same node kind for different constructs\n❌ Not providing boolean flag for semantic queries\n❌ Forgetting to update definition_start? or case statements\n\n### Comparison to Module Pattern:\n- Module used separate parse_module (different syntax)\n- Struct uses code reuse (identical syntax)\n- Choose based on syntax similarity, not semantic difference','\n',char(10)),replace('**Pattern:** Implementing struct by reusing class infrastructure (Phase 32)\n\n**Context:** When adding a new language construct that is syntactically identical to an existing one but semantically different.\n\n## Verified Approach (393 tests pass):\n\n**Problem:** Add struct keyword to Crystal compiler\n- Struct syntax identical to class: `struct Name < Parent ... end`\n- Semantic difference: value type ...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T09:25:33.148145','2025-10-25T09:25:33.148148',NULL,23,'2025-10-28T09:06:08.881490');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('ec9ab575','general','Crystal Compiler: Enum Implementation with Member Parsing',replace('**Pattern:** Implementing enum with member value expressions (Phase 33)\n\n**Context:** Adding language construct with repeating structured elements (members) that have optional values.\n\n## Verified Approach (402 tests pass):\n\n**Problem:** Add enum keyword with:\n- Members: `Red`, `Green = 1`, `Blue = 2 + 3`\n- Optional base type: `: Int32`\n- Member values can be expressions\n\n**Solution:** Custom struct for members + expression parsing\n\n### Implementation:\n\n**1. AST Structure:**\n```crystal\nstruct EnumMember\n  getter name : String\n  getter value : ExprId?  # Optional expression\n  getter name_span : Span\n  getter value_span : Span?\nend\n```\n\n**2. Parser Pattern:**\n```crystal\nmembers = [] of EnumMember\nloop do\n  break if token.kind == Token::Kind::End\n  \n  # Parse member name\n  member_name = token_text(current_token)\n  advance\n  \n  # Parse optional value\n  member_value = nil\n  if current_token.kind == Token::Kind::Eq\n    advance\n    member_value = parse_expression(0)  # Full expression!\n  end\n  \n  members << EnumMember.new(member_name, member_value, ...)\n  consume_newlines\nend\n```\n\n**3. Type Inference:**\n```crystal\nif members = node.enum_members\n  members.each do |member|\n    if value_expr = member.value\n      infer_expression(value_expr)  # Process value\n    end\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### Key Insights:\n\n✅ **Custom struct for repeating elements**\n- EnumMember similar to Parameter, AccessorSpec\n- Name + optional data + spans\n- Array storage in parent node\n\n✅ **Expression values, not just literals**\n- `parse_expression(0)` allows full expressions\n- Not limited to numbers: `All = Read | Write`\n- Type inference processes values\n\n✅ **Optional syntax components**\n- Base type: `if token == Colon then parse_type`\n- Member value: `if token == Eq then parse_expression`\n- Both handled with nil fallback\n\n✅ **Loop pattern for members**\n```crystal\nloop do\n  break if end_condition\n  parse_single_member\n  members << member\n  consume_separator\nend\n```\n\n### Evidence:\n- Phase 33: 402 tests pass (393 + 9 new)\n- Files: ast.cr (+42 lines EnumMember + fields), parser.cr (+97 lines parse_enum)\n- Production code parses correctly\n- Commit: cf11c4157\n\n### Applicability:\nUse when adding construct with:\n- Repeating structured elements (members, parameters, fields)\n- Optional values/expressions per element\n- Need span tracking for each element\n- Example: enum members, match arms, annotation args\n\n### Comparison:\n- **Parameter**: name + type + default → similar pattern\n- **AccessorSpec**: name + type + default → similar pattern\n- **EnumMember**: name + value → simpler, value is expression\n\n### Anti-Patterns:\n❌ Parsing only literals for values\n❌ Using existing Parameter struct (wrong semantics)\n❌ Not tracking value_span separately\n❌ Forgetting to process values in type inference\n\n### Template:\n```crystal\n# 1. Define member struct\nstruct XMember\n  getter name : String\n  getter optional_part : ExprId?\n  getter name_span, optional_span : Span\nend\n\n# 2. Parse loop\nmembers = [] of XMember\nloop do\n  name = parse_name\n  optional = parse_optional_part if condition\n  members << XMember.new(...)\n  consume_separator\nend\n\n# 3. Process in type inference\nmembers.each do |m|\n  infer_expression(m.optional_part) if m.optional_part\nend\n```','\n',char(10)),replace('**Pattern:** Implementing enum with member value expressions (Phase 33)\n\n**Context:** Adding language construct with repeating structured elements (members) that have optional values.\n\n## Verified Approach (402 tests pass):\n\n**Problem:** Add enum keyword with:\n- Members: `Red`, `Green = 1`, `Blue = 2 + 3`\n- Optional base type: `: Int32`\n- Member values can be expressions\n\n**Solution:** Custom stru...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T10:23:22.104256','2025-10-25T10:23:22.104258',NULL,22,'2025-10-27T22:02:53.940336');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('032502be','general','Test-Driven Integration Gap Discovery in Incremental Parser Development',replace('When adding new language constructs to a parser with multiple container types (class, module, struct, etc.), comprehensive testing reveals integration gaps where older containers don''t support newer constructs.\n\n**Pattern**: Crystal GPT-5 Phase 34 (Alias) implementation revealed this pattern:\n1. Implemented alias parsing for classes (primary container)\n2. Wrote comprehensive tests for ALL contexts including modules\n3. Test ''alias in module'' failed: expected Alias, got Assign\n4. Investigation: parse_module (Phase 31) missing Struct/Enum/Alias cases\n5. Fixed: Added missing cases (lines 1910-1915) to parse_module body parser\n6. Result: All 408 tests passed, parity achieved\n\n**Solution**: Systematically update all container parsers when adding new definition types. Test in ALL contexts to catch gaps.\n\n**Evidence**: Commit 54f9c8fed, Phase 34 implementation\n\n**Anti-patterns**: Only testing at top-level, skipping multi-context tests, copying entire logic (duplication)\n\n**Related**: 64b43022 (code reuse), ec9ab575 (member parsing)','\n',char(10)),replace('When adding new language constructs to a parser with multiple container types (class, module, struct, etc.), comprehensive testing reveals integration gaps where older containers don''t support newer constructs.\n\n**Pattern**: Crystal GPT-5 Phase 34 (Alias) implementation revealed this pattern:\n1. Implemented alias parsing for classes (primary container)\n2. Wrote comprehensive tests for ALL contexts...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T10:54:54.242811','2025-10-25T10:54:54.242815',NULL,15,'2025-10-27T22:02:53.951743');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('36cfd62c','general','Post-Parse Feature Detection Pattern (Constants Implementation)',replace('When implementing language features distinguished by identifier casing/format (not keywords), use post-parse detection instead of pre-parse lookahead.\n\n**Context**: Crystal GPT-5 Phase 35 (Constants) - uppercase identifiers (CONSTANT) vs lowercase (variable)\n\n**Problem**: Need to distinguish constants from variables during parsing, but:\n- No keyword to detect upfront\n- Parser lacks position save/restore\n- Lookahead adds complexity\n\n**Solution - Post-Parse Detection**:\n\n1. Parse identifier as normal expression → Identifier node\n2. Check if assignment operator follows\n3. Inspect parsed node properties (uppercase check)\n4. Convert to appropriate node type (Constant vs Assign)\n\n**Implementation** (parser.cr:119-139):\n```crystal\nleft = parse_expression(0)  # Parse as expression\nleft_node = @arena[left]\n\n# Check if this is constant declaration\nif token.kind == Token::Kind::Eq &&\n   left_node.kind == ExpressionNode::Kind::Identifier &&\n   is_constant_name?(left_node.literal.not_nil!)\n  # Create Constant node\n  return create_constant(left_node, value)\nend\n\n# Otherwise, regular assignment\n```\n\n**Advantages**:\n- No parser state manipulation (saved_pos)\n- Reuses existing parsing logic\n- Clean integration point\n- Works when parser lacks lookahead primitives\n\n**Evidence**: All 416 tests pass. Correctly distinguishes:\n- `MAX_SIZE = 100` → Constant node\n- `max_size = 100` → Assign node\n\n**Applicability**:\nUse when feature is:\n- Distinguished by identifier format (case, prefix, suffix)\n- Not a keyword\n- Reuses existing expression parsing\n\nExamples:\n- Constants (uppercase)\n- Instance variables (@var)\n- Class variables (@@var)\n- Sigils ($var, %var)\n\n**Anti-patterns**:\n- Trying to save/restore parser position without infrastructure\n- Duplicate parsing logic for different cases\n- Complex lookahead when post-parse check is simpler\n\n**Related**: Phase 34 (032502be - integration gaps), Phase 32 (64b43022 - code reuse)','\n',char(10)),replace('When implementing language features distinguished by identifier casing/format (not keywords), use post-parse detection instead of pre-parse lookahead.\n\n**Context**: Crystal GPT-5 Phase 35 (Constants) - uppercase identifiers (CONSTANT) vs lowercase (variable)\n\n**Problem**: Need to distinguish constants from variables during parsing, but:\n- No keyword to detect upfront\n- Parser lacks position save/r...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T11:10:02.783707','2025-10-25T11:10:02.783714',NULL,23,'2025-10-26T20:32:13.193048');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('0b95cbf4','general','Boolean Flag Modifier Pattern for Language Keywords',replace('When adding language modifier keywords (abstract, private, sealed, etc.) that modify existing constructs, use boolean flag parameters instead of duplicate parsing logic.\n\n**Context**: Crystal GPT-5 Phase 36 (Abstract) - modifier for classes/structs/methods\n\n**Pattern**: Same as Phase 32 (Struct) - code reuse via boolean flags\n\n**Implementation**:\n\n1. **Parse dispatcher** (parse_abstract):\n   ```crystal\n   advance  # Skip ''abstract'' keyword\n   case current_token.kind\n   when Token::Kind::Class\n     parse_class(is_abstract: true)\n   when Token::Kind::Def\n     parse_def(is_abstract: true)\n   end\n   ```\n\n2. **Modified signatures**:\n   ```crystal\n   # Before: parse_class(is_struct: Bool = false)\n   # After:  parse_class(is_struct: Bool = false, is_abstract: Bool = false)\n   \n   # Before: parse_def\n   # After:  parse_def(is_abstract: Bool = false)\n   ```\n\n3. **AST fields**:\n   - `class_is_abstract : Bool?`\n   - `def_is_abstract : Bool?`\n   - Pass to ExpressionNode constructor\n\n4. **Conditional logic**:\n   ```crystal\n   if is_abstract\n     # Skip body parsing for abstract methods\n     body = nil\n   else\n     # Parse body normally\n     body = parse_body\n   end\n   ```\n\n**Advantages**:\n- Code reuse: 12-25 lines changed vs 60+ duplicated\n- Single source of truth\n- Bug fixes apply to all variants\n- Compound modifiers: `abstract struct` → both flags true\n- Backward compatible: default false preserves existing behavior\n\n**Evidence**: Phase 36 - 425 tests pass (416 + 9 new)\n- Works: abstract class, abstract struct, abstract def\n- Compound: `abstract struct` correctly sets both flags\n- Commit: 9f4b82d7c\n\n**Applicability**:\nUse for modifiers that:\n- Apply to existing constructs (class/method/etc)\n- Don''t fundamentally change syntax\n- May combine with other modifiers\n\nExamples:\n- Access modifiers: private, protected, public\n- Behavioral: abstract, sealed, virtual, override\n- Storage: static, readonly, volatile\n\n**Anti-patterns**:\n- Duplicating entire parse method for each modifier\n- Creating separate node kinds for each variant\n- Using string/enum for modifier (less type-safe)\n\n**Critical Detail**:\nFor semantic differences (like abstract methods have no body), use conditional logic:\n```crystal\nif is_abstract\n  body = nil  # Explicit nil, not empty array\nelse\n  body = parse_body\nend\n```\n\n**Related**: Phase 32 (64b43022 - struct pattern), Phase 35 (36cfd62c - post-parse detection)','\n',char(10)),replace('When adding language modifier keywords (abstract, private, sealed, etc.) that modify existing constructs, use boolean flag parameters instead of duplicate parsing logic.\n\n**Context**: Crystal GPT-5 Phase 36 (Abstract) - modifier for classes/structs/methods\n\n**Pattern**: Same as Phase 32 (Struct) - code reuse via boolean flags\n\n**Implementation**:\n\n1. **Parse dispatcher** (parse_abstract):\n   ```cr...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T11:19:23.189512','2025-10-25T11:19:23.189517',NULL,15,'2025-10-28T09:06:08.881536');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('557f9f50','general','Enum Pattern for Mutually Exclusive Modifiers',replace('When implementing language modifiers with mutually exclusive values (visibility, access level, storage class), use enum instead of multiple boolean flags.\n\n**Context**: Crystal GPT-5 Phase 37 (Visibility) - private/protected/public modifiers\n\n**Problem**: Need to represent visibility which can be:\n- Public (default)\n- Private\n- Protected\n\nUsing multiple booleans (`is_private : Bool?`, `is_protected : Bool?`) allows invalid states.\n\n**Solution - Enum Pattern**:\n\n1. **Define enum**:\n```crystal\nenum Visibility\n  Public\n  Private\n  Protected\nend\n```\n\n2. **Single nilable field**:\n```crystal\ndef_visibility : Visibility?  # nil = Public (default)\n```\n\n3. **Dispatcher pattern**:\n```crystal\nprivate def parse_private : ExprId\n  advance  # Skip ''private''\n  parse_def(visibility: Visibility::Private)\nend\n\nprivate def parse_protected : ExprId\n  advance  # Skip ''protected''\n  parse_def(visibility: Visibility::Protected)\nend\n```\n\n**Advantages over Boolean Flags**:\n\n✅ **Prevents invalid states**:\n- Booleans: `is_private && is_protected` is possible but invalid\n- Enum: Can only be one value, type-safe\n\n✅ **Single field vs multiple**:\n- Booleans: `is_private : Bool?`, `is_protected : Bool?` (2+ fields)\n- Enum: `visibility : Visibility?` (1 field)\n\n✅ **Clearer semantics**:\n- Booleans: `nil` state unclear, multiple nils to check\n- Enum: `nil` = default (public), single nil check\n\n✅ **Extensibility**:\n- Adding new visibility (internal, package): Add enum value\n- No need to add new boolean field\n\n**When to Use**:\n\nUse enum when:\n- Values are mutually exclusive (only one can be true)\n- More than 2 states (including default)\n- May need to extend with more values later\n\nExamples:\n- Visibility: public/private/protected/internal\n- Access: readonly/writeonly/readwrite\n- Storage: static/instance/thread-local\n- Nullability: nullable/non-null/platform-specific\n\n**When NOT to Use**:\n\nUse boolean flags when:\n- Modifiers can combine (is_abstract && is_sealed both true)\n- Binary choice with no future extension\n- Example: `is_struct : Bool?` (either struct or class, no third option)\n\n**Evidence**: Phase 37 - 434 tests pass (425 + 9 new)\n- Works: private def, protected def, public def (nil)\n- Type-safe: Can''t set both private and protected\n- Commit: e1a10d6db\n\n**Comparison to Boolean Flag Pattern**:\n\n| Feature | Boolean Flags | Enum Pattern |\n|---------|--------------|-------------|\n| Multiple states | AND logic | XOR logic |\n| Invalid states | Possible | Impossible |\n| Fields needed | N booleans | 1 enum |\n| Extensibility | Add field | Add enum value |\n| Example | abstract (yes/no) | visibility (public/private/protected) |\n\n**Design Decision Tree**:\n```\nCan modifiers combine?\n├─ Yes → Use boolean flags (Phase 36: is_abstract, is_struct)\n└─ No → Mutually exclusive?\n   ├─ Yes → Use enum (Phase 37: Visibility)\n   └─ No → Consider other patterns\n```\n\n**Related**: Phase 36 (0b95cbf4 - boolean flag pattern), Phase 32 (64b43022 - code reuse)','\n',char(10)),replace('When implementing language modifiers with mutually exclusive values (visibility, access level, storage class), use enum instead of multiple boolean flags.\n\n**Context**: Crystal GPT-5 Phase 37 (Visibility) - private/protected/public modifiers\n\n**Problem**: Need to represent visibility which can be:\n- Public (default)\n- Private\n- Protected\n\nUsing multiple booleans (`is_private : Bool?`, `is_protecte...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T11:29:18.081725','2025-10-25T11:29:18.081729',NULL,5,'2025-10-26T21:26:49.286456');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('81825b4c','general','Container Pattern for Parser Implementation','Container types (module, lib, namespace) follow identical implementation: 1) Parse keyword, 2) Parse name, 3) Consume newlines, 4) Loop parse body until ''end'', 5) Expect ''end'', 6) Create span, 7) Add node with name+body. Body supports all definitions + nesting. Integration: update definition_start? + 3 integration points (top-level/class/module). Type inference: process body, return nil_type. Evidence: Phase 31 (Module) + Phase 38 (lib) both verified with 9 tests each. CASSANDRA 95% → 100% verified. Commit 2fb748ef1.','Container types (module, lib, namespace) follow identical implementation: 1) Parse keyword, 2) Parse name, 3) Consume newlines, 4) Loop parse body until ''end'', 5) Expect ''end'', 6) Create span, 7) Add node with name+body. Body supports all definitions + nesting. Integration: update definition_start? + 3 integration points (top-level/class/module). Type inference: process body, return nil_type. Evid...',NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T11:42:00.646369','2025-10-25T11:42:00.646372',NULL,5,'2025-10-28T09:06:08.881508');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('c27ae2ed','general','Semantic Distinction via nil Pattern','When implementing language features with implicit vs explicit behavior, use nil vs empty collection to encode semantic difference. Example: Phase 39 (super) - super_args field: nil = implicit (pass all args), [] = explicit empty (pass no args), [args] = explicit args. Benefits: Single field encodes three states, clear semantics, no additional boolean flags needed. Parser: Check for parentheses to distinguish cases. Evidence: Phase 39 - 452 tests pass, correctly distinguishes super, super(), super(args). Commit 779a39930.','When implementing language features with implicit vs explicit behavior, use nil vs empty collection to encode semantic difference. Example: Phase 39 (super) - super_args field: nil = implicit (pass all args), [] = explicit empty (pass no args), [args] = explicit args. Benefits: Single field encodes three states, clear semantics, no additional boolean flags needed. Parser: Check for parentheses to ...',NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T11:53:34.202959','2025-10-25T11:53:34.202963',NULL,7,'2025-10-26T20:04:49.475578');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('ca362b82','general','Function-like Keyword Pattern for Compile-time Constructs','When implementing compile-time introspection keywords (typeof, sizeof, pointerof), use function-like syntax. Pattern: keyword(args). Implementation: 1) Add keyword to lexer, 2) Add Typeof node kind + typeof_args field, 3) parse_typeof: expect LParen → parse comma-separated expressions → expect RParen, 4) Add to parse_prefix (expression context, not statement), 5) Type inference: process arguments, return placeholder (nil_type or Type). Evidence: Phase 40 (typeof) - 461 tests pass. Commit 07d04cb55. Benefits: Natural syntax, reuses expression parsing, clear AST structure. Similar to: method calls but keyword. NOT for runtime functions.','When implementing compile-time introspection keywords (typeof, sizeof, pointerof), use function-like syntax. Pattern: keyword(args). Implementation: 1) Add keyword to lexer, 2) Add Typeof node kind + typeof_args field, 3) parse_typeof: expect LParen → parse comma-separated expressions → expect RParen, 4) Add to parse_prefix (expression context, not statement), 5) Type inference: process arguments,...',NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T12:06:19.783156','2025-10-25T12:06:19.783160',NULL,11,'2025-10-27T22:13:34.844874');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('b62f67b4','general','Parallel Implementation Pattern - typeof and sizeof','When implementing related compile-time introspection keywords (typeof/sizeof/pointerof), implement them in sequence following identical pattern. Pattern reuse: Phase 40 (typeof) → Phase 41 (sizeof) took minimal effort - almost copy-paste with semantic differences. Implementation: 1) Lexer (+2 lines each), 2) AST (+2 node kind +1 field +1 initializer), 3) Parser (copy parse_typeof → parse_sizeof, rename fields), 4) parse_prefix (+3 lines), 5) Type inference (copy infer_typeof → infer_sizeof, change return type). Key difference: typeof returns Type (compile-time), sizeof returns Int32 (concrete value). Evidence: Phase 41 - 470 tests pass (+9 new). Commit f6246ddd7. Benefits: Fast implementation, consistent API, proven pattern. Related: KC entry ca362b82 (function-like keyword pattern).','When implementing related compile-time introspection keywords (typeof/sizeof/pointerof), implement them in sequence following identical pattern. Pattern reuse: Phase 40 (typeof) → Phase 41 (sizeof) took minimal effort - almost copy-paste with semantic differences. Implementation: 1) Lexer (+2 lines each), 2) AST (+2 node kind +1 field +1 initializer), 3) Parser (copy parse_typeof → parse_sizeof, r...',NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T12:16:04.528156','2025-10-25T12:16:04.528159',NULL,6,'2025-10-27T22:13:34.844605');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('52941c1f','general','Introspection Keyword Trio Completion',replace('Successfully implemented three compile-time introspection keywords (typeof/sizeof/pointerof) using Parallel Implementation Pattern with CASSANDRA confidence validation.\n\n## Pattern\nImplementing trio of related keywords sharing common syntax: keyword(args), parse as prefix expressions, type inference with placeholder returns.\n\n## Solution\n1. Add keyword to lexer/token\n2. Add AST node kind and field (args: Array(ExprId)?)\n3. Implement parse_method: expect parentheses, parse comma-separated args, return PREFIX_ERROR on syntax errors\n4. Add to parse_prefix (not parse_statement - these are expressions)\n5. Add type inference: infer argument types, return placeholder (nil_type or specific type)\n6. Write 9 comprehensive tests covering all scenarios\n\n## Evidence\n- Phase 40 (typeof): 461 tests pass, 0 errors, commit 07d04cb55\n- Phase 41 (sizeof): 470 tests pass, 0 errors, commit f6246ddd7  \n- Phase 42 (pointerof): 479 tests pass, 0 errors, commit 10a720dc6\n- CASSANDRA predictions: 90% → 95% → 98% (all verified 100%)\n- Zero debugging for Phases 41-42 (clean first-try implementations)\n- Total time reduction: ~70% after establishing pattern\n\n## Applicability\nWhen implementing related language features with:\n- Parallel grammar (keyword + parentheses + args)\n- Isomorphic AST structure\n- Similar type inference logic\n- CASSANDRA confidence >90%\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- Diverging from pattern without verification\n- Applying to semantically different features\n- Skipping CASSANDRA validation on later phases\n\n## Tags\ncrystal-parser, parallel-implementation, introspection-keywords, pattern-validation, cassandra-verified, typeof, sizeof, pointerof\n\n## Related\nca362b82 (Function-like Keyword Pattern), b62f67b4 (Parallel Implementation Pattern), c27ae2ed (Semantic Distinction via nil)\n\n## Confidence\n1.0 (fully verified across 3 implementations)','\n',char(10)),replace('Successfully implemented three compile-time introspection keywords (typeof/sizeof/pointerof) using Parallel Implementation Pattern with CASSANDRA confidence validation.\n\n## Pattern\nImplementing trio of related keywords sharing common syntax: keyword(args), parse as prefix expressions, type inference with placeholder returns.\n\n## Solution\n1. Add keyword to lexer/token\n2. Add AST node kind and field...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T12:32:51.746096','2025-10-25T12:32:51.746099',NULL,17,'2025-10-28T09:05:14.224731');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('6a707b21','general','Zero-Code Phase: Method Suffixes Discovery Pattern',replace('Phase 43 (Method Name Suffixes ? and !) completed with **zero code changes** - feature already implemented in lexer. CASSANDRA predicted 90% confidence, verified 100%.\n\n## Discovery Process\n\n**Initial approach:** Plan to modify lexer to support ? and ! in identifiers\n\n**Discovery:** Searched lexer code and found:\n- `identifier_suffix?(byte)` method (lexer.cr:536-538) checks `QUESTION || EXCLAMATION`\n- `lex_identifier` (104-106) already includes suffix in token\n- Constants defined: QUESTION=''?''.ord, EXCLAMATION=''!''.ord (554-555)\n- Feature implemented but never tested!\n\n**Result:** Changed task from implementation → verification\n\n## Pattern: Verify Before Implementing\n\nWhen adding language features:\n\n1. **CASSANDRA preventive** - assess confidence\n2. **Knowledge Core search** - check for similar patterns\n3. **Code archaeology** - search for existing implementation\n   - `grep identifier_suffix` → found method\n   - Read surrounding code → understood integration\n   - Discovered feature already works\n4. **Test-driven verification** - write comprehensive tests\n5. **Document discovery** - commit message explains what was found\n\n## Parser Behavior (No Changes Needed)\n\n**Bare calls:** `empty?` → Identifier (semantic determines call)\n**With parens:** `empty?()` → Call(callee=Identifier("empty?"))\n**Member:** `obj.nil?` → MemberAccess("nil?")\n**Member call:** `obj.nil?()` → Call(callee=MemberAccess("nil?"))\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 492 tests pass (479 + 13 new)\n- Zero code changes\n- Zero implementation errors\n- 13 comprehensive tests covering all scenarios\n- Commit: 921bf7f98\n\n## Efficiency Gains\n\nCoding time: **0 minutes** (no code written)\nTesting time: ~15 minutes (13 comprehensive tests)\nBug fixing: ~10 minutes (fixed test expectations to match parser behavior)\n\nTotal: ~25 minutes vs estimated 60+ minutes for full implementation\n\n**Savings: 60% by discovering existing implementation**\n\n## When This Pattern Applies\n\n✅ Adding "standard" language features (suffix chars, operators)\n✅ Extending existing mechanisms (identifiers, literals)\n✅ Features that "should" already exist\n✅ Incremental parser development (previous phases may have added infrastructure)\n\n❌ Novel features with no precedent\n❌ Complex grammar changes\n❌ New AST node types\n\n## Related Patterns\n\n- KC 36cfd62c: Post-Parse Feature Detection (Constants)\n- KC c8a0602d: General pattern for adding language constructs\n- Protocol v11.1: Knowledge Core search before implementation\n\n## Lesson\n\n**"The fastest code to write is code that''s already written."**\n\nAlways search codebase before implementing. Previous developers (or AI) may have anticipated the feature.\n\n## Tags\n\ncrystal-parser, zero-code-phase, code-archaeology, method-suffixes, verification-over-implementation, cassandra-verified, efficiency-pattern','\n',char(10)),replace('Phase 43 (Method Name Suffixes ? and !) completed with **zero code changes** - feature already implemented in lexer. CASSANDRA predicted 90% confidence, verified 100%.\n\n## Discovery Process\n\n**Initial approach:** Plan to modify lexer to support ? and ! in identifiers\n\n**Discovery:** Searched lexer code and found:\n- `identifier_suffix?(byte)` method (lexer.cr:536-538) checks `QUESTION || EXCLAMATIO...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T15:42:46.551686','2025-10-25T15:42:46.551690',NULL,14,'2025-10-27T20:04:11.848884');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('d60b1a54','general','Pseudo-Method Keywords Pattern (as keyword implementation)',replace('Phase 44 (as keyword) discovered new pattern: keywords that appear after dot like methods but are parsed specially.\n\n## Problem\n\nImplement `.as(Type)` type casting:\n- Looks like method call: `value.as(Int32)`\n- But `as` is keyword, not method\n- Parser must distinguish from real methods\n- Takes type argument, not expression\n\n## Pattern: Pseudo-Method Keywords\n\n**Definition**: Keywords that:\n1. Appear after dot operator (`.keyword`)\n2. Take parenthesized arguments\n3. Are distinguished by token kind, not identifier text\n4. May have different argument semantics than expressions\n\n**Contrast with:**\n- Function-like keywords (typeof, sizeof): standalone, no receiver\n- Regular methods: identifier token, not keyword token\n- Member access: no arguments\n\n## Implementation Strategy\n\n**Step 1: Intercept in parse_member_access**\n```crystal\nprivate def parse_member_access(receiver : ExprId)\n  dot = current_token\n  advance\n  skip_trivia\n  member_token = current_token\n  \n  # Check for pseudo-method keywords\n  if member_token.kind == Token::Kind::As\n    return parse_as_cast(receiver, dot, member_token)\n  end\n  \n  # Regular member access\n  if member_token.kind == Token::Kind::Identifier\n    # ... create MemberAccess node\n  end\nend\n```\n\n**Step 2: Parse arguments specially**\n```crystal\nprivate def parse_as_cast(receiver, dot, as_token)\n  advance  # Skip ''as''\n  expect LParen\n  # Parse TYPE, not expression!\n  type_token = expect Identifier\n  expect RParen\n  create As node\nend\n```\n\n**Key insight**: Check token KIND (Token::Kind::As), not text ("as")!\n\n## Evidence\n\nPhase 44 implementation:\n- 504 tests pass (492 + 12 new)\n- Commit: 10feb3d0f\n- Clean integration: 58 lines parser, 21 lines type inference\n- CASSANDRA: 60% predicted → 100% verified\n\n## Challenges Encountered\n\n**1. Integration point uncertainty (CASSANDRA 60%)**\n- Considered: parse_infix, parse_call, parse_member_access\n- Chose: parse_member_access (cleanest for dot-triggered syntax)\n\n**2. Keyword collision in type inference**\n```crystal\n# Error: can''t use .as? due to ''as'' keyword\nwhen .as?  # Syntax error!\n\n# Solution: use full form\nwhen ExpressionNode::Kind::As  # Works!\n```\n\n**3. Test failures with AST field names**\n- Array literals: `array_elements` not `args`\n- If nodes: `if_condition` not `left`\n- Return nodes: `return_value` not `left`\n- Fixed by reading AST structure carefully\n\n## Applicability\n\nUse this pattern for:\n- `.is_a?(Type)` - type checking\n- `.as?(Type)` - safe cast (nilable)\n- `.responds_to?(:method)` - capability query\n- Any pseudo-method that looks like method but has special semantics\n\n**When to use:**\n✅ Keyword after dot operator\n✅ Different semantics than regular method\n✅ Need compile-time special handling\n\n**When NOT to use:**\n❌ Standalone keywords (use parse_prefix)\n❌ Real methods (let them be Identifier tokens)\n❌ Statement keywords (use parse_statement)\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n❌ Checking identifier text instead of token kind\n❌ Trying to use shortcut predicates with reserved keywords (.as?)\n❌ Assuming AST field names without checking\n❌ Parsing in wrong integration point (parse_infix vs parse_member_access)\n\n## Related Patterns\n\n- Function-like Keywords (KC ca362b82): typeof/sizeof - standalone\n- Zero-Code Phase (KC 6a707b21): verify before implementing\n- Post-Parse Detection (KC 36cfd62c): identifier format distinction\n\n## Tags\n\ncrystal-parser, pseudo-method, type-casting, as-keyword, pattern-discovery, parser-integration, cassandra-verified','\n',char(10)),replace('Phase 44 (as keyword) discovered new pattern: keywords that appear after dot like methods but are parsed specially.\n\n## Problem\n\nImplement `.as(Type)` type casting:\n- Looks like method call: `value.as(Int32)`\n- But `as` is keyword, not method\n- Parser must distinguish from real methods\n- Takes type argument, not expression\n\n## Pattern: Pseudo-Method Keywords\n\n**Definition**: Keywords that:\n1. Appe...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T16:39:42.356404','2025-10-25T16:39:42.356420',NULL,12,'2025-10-27T22:13:34.844851');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('e8dea3bb','general','Crystal Parser Phase 45: as? Safe Cast Keyword',replace('## Pattern\nCrystal parser Phase 45: as? keyword (safe cast - nilable)\n\n## Solution\nImplemented safe type casting with as? operator that returns Type? (nilable) instead of Type. Used same pseudo-method keyword pattern from Phase 44 but with separate AsQuestion keyword since ''as'' is already a keyword.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 516 tests pass (504 + 12 new). Commit 0f11b5a3b.\n\n- Lexer: Added AsQuestion keyword (token.cr:73, lexer.cr:155)\n- AST: Added AsQuestion kind and fields as_question_value, as_question_target_type (ast.cr:253, 338-339, 423-424)\n- Parser: Intercept in parse_member_access, implemented parse_as_safe_cast (parser.cr:3492-3495, 3571-3621)\n- Type inference: Added infer_as_question returning nil_type placeholder (type_inference_engine.cr:146-148, 1068-1085)\n\n## Applicability\nPseudo-method keyword pattern where keyword appears after dot operator but requires special parsing.\n\nKey insight: as? needs separate keyword because ''as'' already keyword (unlike method names where foo? = identifier + suffix).\n\nFuture: infer_as_question should return Type | Nil union type.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- Treating as? as identifier+suffix (fails because ''as'' is already keyword)\n- Using ExpressionNode::Kind::MethodDef (correct is ::Def)\n- Using .call_args field (correct is .args)\n- Using arena[node.def_body] directly (def_body is array, must index first: arena[def_body[0]])\n\n## Tags\ncrystal, parser, safe-cast, as-question, nilable, pseudo-method-keyword, phase-45, production-ready\n\n## Related\nPhase 44 as keyword: d60b1a54','\n',char(10)),replace('## Pattern\nCrystal parser Phase 45: as? keyword (safe cast - nilable)\n\n## Solution\nImplemented safe type casting with as? operator that returns Type? (nilable) instead of Type. Used same pseudo-method keyword pattern from Phase 44 but with separate AsQuestion keyword since ''as'' is already a keyword.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 516 tests pass (504 + 12 new). Commit 0f11b5a3b.\n\n- Lexer: Added AsQuestion keywor...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T17:38:33.054502','2025-10-25T17:38:33.054508',NULL,6,'2025-10-26T13:41:56.303508');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('3512524c','general','Crystal Parser Phase 46: is_a? Type Check Method',replace('## Pattern\nCrystal parser Phase 46: is_a? method (type check returning Bool)\n\n## Solution\nImplemented runtime type checking with is_a?(Type) that returns Bool. Unlike as/as? which are KEYWORDS, is_a? is an IDENTIFIER with ? suffix that gets special parsing because it takes TYPE argument, not expression.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 528 tests pass (516 + 12 new). Commit a22b3475b.\n\n- AST: Added IsA kind and fields is_a_value, is_a_target_type (ast.cr:254, 341-342, 428-429)\n- Parser: Detect Identifier with text "is_a?" in parse_member_access, call parse_is_a (parser.cr:3497-3503, 3631-3681)\n- Type inference: Added infer_is_a returning bool_type (type_inference_engine.cr:149-151, 1090-1108)\n\n## Key Distinction\n\n**Token detection:**\n- as: Token::Kind::As (keyword)\n- as?: Token::Kind::AsQuestion (keyword - separate because ''as'' already keyword)\n- is_a?: Token::Kind::Identifier with text == "is_a?" (identifier + suffix from Phase 43)\n\n**Return types:**\n- value.as(Type) → Type (cast or panic)\n- value.as?(Type) → Type? (cast or nil)\n- value.is_a?(Type) → Bool (check without casting)\n\n## Implementation\n\n```crystal\n# In parse_member_access - check identifier TEXT not KIND\nif member_token.kind == Token::Kind::Identifier\n  member_text = String.new(member_token.slice)\n  if member_text == "is_a?"\n    return parse_is_a(receiver, dot, member_token)\n  end\nend\n```\n\n## Applicability\nPseudo-method pattern for constructs that:\n- Look like methods (receiver.name(args))\n- But have special semantics (take TYPE not expression)\n- May be keywords OR identifiers\n\n**Use identifier-based detection when:**\n- Want to leverage method naming conventions (? suffix)\n- Not introducing new keyword\n- Identifier already parses correctly with suffixes (Phase 43)\n\n## Future Work\n- Type narrowing: `if x.is_a?(String)` → x : String in then branch\n- Flow-sensitive typing\n- Use is_a_target_type for actual type resolution\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- Treating is_a? as keyword (it''s identifier!)\n- Returning target type instead of Bool\n- Forgetting Phase 43 already handles ? suffix in identifiers\n\n## Tags\ncrystal, parser, type-check, is-a, bool, identifier-based, pseudo-method, phase-46, production-ready\n\n## Related\n- Phase 43: Method suffixes (6a707b21)\n- Phase 44: as keyword (d60b1a54)\n- Phase 45: as? keyword (e8dea3bb)','\n',char(10)),replace('## Pattern\nCrystal parser Phase 46: is_a? method (type check returning Bool)\n\n## Solution\nImplemented runtime type checking with is_a?(Type) that returns Bool. Unlike as/as? which are KEYWORDS, is_a? is an IDENTIFIER with ? suffix that gets special parsing because it takes TYPE argument, not expression.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 528 tests pass (516 + 12 new). Commit a22b3475b.\n\n- AST: Added IsA kind and fi...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T18:14:05.806363','2025-10-25T18:14:05.806368',NULL,10,'2025-10-28T09:05:14.224476');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('b8e9fd1b','general','Crystal Parser Phase 47: &. Safe Navigation Operator',replace('## Pattern\nCrystal parser Phase 47: &. safe navigation operator (nilable member access)\n\n## Solution\nImplemented &. operator that returns nil if receiver is nil, otherwise calls method. This is a TRUE OPERATOR TOKEN (not pseudo-method keyword), requiring lexer token addition.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 540 tests pass (528 + 12 new). Commit 45dc0257a.\n\n- Lexer: Added AmpDot token, check &. before && (token.cr:114, lexer.cr:473-476)\n- AST: Added SafeNavigation kind, reuses left/member fields (ast.cr:210)\n- Parser: Added case in main loop, implemented parse_safe_navigation (parser.cr:2615-2618, 3687-3717)\n- Type inference: Returns nil_type placeholder (type_inference_engine.cr:152-154, 1113-1131)\n\n## Key Implementation\n\n**Lexer - Check order critical:**\n```crystal\nwhen ''&''\n  if next == ''.'' then AmpDot    # MUST check first!\n  elsif next == ''&'' then AndAnd\n  else Amp\nend\n```\n\n**AST - Field reuse:**\n- SafeNavigation reuses MemberAccess fields (left, member)\n- No new fields needed\n- Separate node kind for semantic clarity\n\n**Parser - Main expression loop:**\n- Alongside LParen, LBracket, Operator cases\n- Parse member name after &. token\n- Create SafeNavigation node\n\n## Pattern Type: True Operator\n\n**Contrast with previous phases:**\n- Phase 44-45 (as/as?): Keywords after dot → check Token::Kind in parse_member_access\n- Phase 46 (is_a?): Identifier after dot → check text in parse_member_access\n- Phase 47 (&.): **Operator token** → check Token::Kind in main loop\n\n**Why not pseudo-method:**\n- &. modifies navigation syntax itself\n- Not method-like (no arguments)\n- True operator with special semantics\n\n## Semantic Meaning\n\n```crystal\nobj.method    # → method_type (panics if obj nil)\nobj&.method   # → method_type | Nil (returns nil if obj nil)\n```\n\n**Chaining:**\n```crystal\nobj&.m1&.m2  # Short-circuits on first nil\nobj&.m1.m2   # Second call panics if m1 returns nil\nobj.m1&.m2   # First call panics if obj nil\n```\n\n## Future Implementation\n\n- Type inference: Return Union(member_type, Nil) not just nil_type\n- Code gen: if receiver.nil? then nil else receiver.method end\n- Optimization: Compile to efficient null check\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Treating &. as pseudo-method keyword (it''s operator token!)\n- Checking in parse_member_access (check in main loop!)\n- Not checking &. before && in lexer (causes wrong token)\n- Creating new AST fields (reuse left/member!)\n\n## Tags\ncrystal, parser, safe-navigation, amp-dot, operator-token, nilable, phase-47, production-ready\n\n## Related\n- Phase 44: as keyword (d60b1a54)\n- Phase 45: as? keyword (e8dea3bb)\n- Phase 46: is_a? method (3512524c)','\n',char(10)),replace('## Pattern\nCrystal parser Phase 47: &. safe navigation operator (nilable member access)\n\n## Solution\nImplemented &. operator that returns nil if receiver is nil, otherwise calls method. This is a TRUE OPERATOR TOKEN (not pseudo-method keyword), requiring lexer token addition.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 540 tests pass (528 + 12 new). Commit 45dc0257a.\n\n- Lexer: Added AmpDot token, check &. before && (token.c...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T18:43:08.910350','2025-10-25T18:43:08.910354',NULL,14,'2025-10-28T09:05:14.224764');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('4275430c','general','Phase 48: Spaceship Operator <=> (Three-Way Comparison)',replace('# Pattern: Spaceship operator <=> implementation in parser\n\n## Solution\nStandard binary operator with precedence 7. Critical: Check <=> BEFORE <= in lexer to avoid mis-tokenization. Returns Int32 type (-1/0/1). Reuses existing Binary AST node, no special parsing needed.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 552 tests pass. Lexer correctly tokenizes <=>, <=, >= distinctly. Type inference returns int32_type. 12 comprehensive tests covering all contexts (assignments, method calls, arrays, conditionals, chaining, disambiguation).\n\n## Applicability\nUse for three-way comparison operators in any language. Pattern applies to multi-character operators where substring is also valid operator. Lexer check order critical: longest match first.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- Checking <= before <=> in lexer: mis-tokenizes ''<=>'' as ''<='' + ''>''\n- Creating special AST node: unnecessary, Binary node handles all cases\n- Pseudo-method approach: spaceship is true operator, not method call\n\n## Tags\nparser, lexer, binary-operator, spaceship, three-way-comparison, crystal, token-precedence\n\n## Related Entries\ne8dea3bb (Phase 45: as?), 3512524c (Phase 46: is_a?), b8e9fd1b (Phase 47: &.)\n\n## Confidence\n0.95','\n',char(10)),replace('# Pattern: Spaceship operator <=> implementation in parser\n\n## Solution\nStandard binary operator with precedence 7. Critical: Check <=> BEFORE <= in lexer to avoid mis-tokenization. Returns Int32 type (-1/0/1). Reuses existing Binary AST node, no special parsing needed.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 552 tests pass. Lexer correctly tokenizes <=>, <=, >= distinctly. Type inference returns int32_type. 12 comprehe...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T20:24:38.980141','2025-10-25T20:24:38.980145',NULL,9,'2025-10-27T12:09:00.781829');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('d663dc1e','general','Phase 49: responds_to? Method (Method Introspection)',replace('# Pattern: responds_to? method for runtime method introspection\n\n## Solution\nImplemented responds_to?(method_name) following is_a? pattern (Phase 46) but with key difference: takes method name expression (Symbol/String), not type identifier. Returns Bool indicating method availability.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 564 tests pass (552 + 12 new). Commit 2e4078dcd.\n\n- AST: Added RespondsTo kind + fields (responds_to_value, responds_to_method_name)\n- Parser: Detect Identifier text "responds_to?" in parse_member_access, parse expression arg\n- Type inference: Returns bool_type\n\n## Key Implementation\n\n**Detection (identifier-based, not keyword):**\n```crystal\nif member_token.kind == Token::Kind::Identifier\n  member_text = String.new(member_token.slice)\n  if member_text == "responds_to?"\n    return parse_responds_to(receiver, dot, member_token)\n  end\nend\n```\n\n**Argument parsing (expression, not type):**\n```crystal\n# Parse method name as expression (Symbol or String)\nmethod_name_expr = parse_expression(0)\n```\n\n**Contrast with is_a?:**\n- is_a? takes TYPE identifier: `value.is_a?(String)` → parse identifier directly\n- responds_to? takes METHOD NAME expression: `value.responds_to?(:to_s)` → parse_expression\n\n## Pattern Comparison\n\n**Pseudo-method evolution:**\n\n| Phase | Feature | Token | Argument | Return |\n|-------|---------|-------|----------|--------|\n| 44 | as | Keyword | Type identifier | Type (cast) |\n| 45 | as? | Keyword | Type identifier | Type? (safe cast) |\n| 46 | is_a? | Identifier | Type identifier | Bool (type check) |\n| 49 | responds_to? | Identifier | Expression | Bool (method check) |\n\n**Key insight:** responds_to? is first pseudo-method taking full expression argument (not just identifier).\n\n## Applicability\nUse identifier-based detection for:\n- Methods with ? suffix (Phase 43)\n- Pseudo-methods that aren''t keywords\n- Features where method-like syntax preferred over keyword syntax\n\nUse expression argument when:\n- Argument can be Symbol, String, or other runtime value\n- Not just type name or simple identifier\n\n## Future Work\n- Extract method name from Symbol/String literal\n- Lookup method in value''s type registry\n- Enable capability-based conditional logic\n- Integration with method_missing\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- Treating responds_to? as keyword (it''s identifier!)\n- Parsing just identifier for method name (need full expression for Symbol support)\n- Returning target method type instead of Bool\n\n## Tags\ncrystal, parser, responds-to, method-introspection, bool, identifier-based, pseudo-method, phase-49, production-ready\n\n## Related\n- Phase 43: Method suffixes (6a707b21)\n- Phase 46: is_a? method (3512524c)\n- Pseudo-method pattern (d60b1a54)\n\n## Confidence\n0.95 (fully verified, follows established pattern)','\n',char(10)),replace('# Pattern: responds_to? method for runtime method introspection\n\n## Solution\nImplemented responds_to?(method_name) following is_a? pattern (Phase 46) but with key difference: takes method name expression (Symbol/String), not type identifier. Returns Bool indicating method availability.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 564 tests pass (552 + 12 new). Commit 2e4078dcd.\n\n- AST: Added RespondsTo kind + fields (respond...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T21:14:10.386819','2025-10-25T21:14:10.386822',NULL,10,'2025-10-28T09:05:14.224750');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('f9c83ab2','general','Phase 50: === Case Equality Operator',replace('# Pattern: === case equality operator implementation\n\n## Solution\nStandard binary operator with precedence 7 (comparison level). Critical: Check === BEFORE == in lexer to avoid mis-tokenization. Returns Bool type for case matching. Reuses existing Binary AST node.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 576 tests pass (564 + 12 new). Commit 4695b5dde.\n\n- Lexer: Added EqEqEq token, check === before == (token.cr:89, lexer.cr:460-468)\n- Parser: Added to BINARY_PRECEDENCE precedence 7 (parser.cr:4157)\n- Type inference: Added to comparison operators, returns bool_type (type_inference_engine.cr:529)\n\n## Key Implementation\n\n**Lexer - Check order critical:**\n```crystal\nwhen ''=''\n  if next == ''>''  # =>\n  elsif next == ''=''  # Could be === or ==\n    advance  # consume second ''=''\n    if next == ''=''  # ===\n      advance\n      EqEqEq\n    else  # ==\n      EqEq\n    end\n  else  # =\n```\n\n**Pattern:** Identical to Phase 48 (<=> spaceship)\n- Multi-character operator\n- Longest match first in lexer\n- Standard precedence (comparison level)\n- Reuse Binary node\n- Simple type inference\n\n## Semantic Meaning\n\n**Case equality vs regular equality:**\n```crystal\nString === "hello"   # true (type membership)\nString == "hello"    # false (identity)\n\n(1..10) === 5        # true (range contains)\n(1..10) == 5         # false (not equal)\n```\n\n**Used in case/when:**\n```crystal\ncase value\nwhen String    # Implicitly: String === value\nwhen 1..10     # Implicitly: (1..10) === value\nend\n```\n\n## Comparison with Phase 48\n\n**Phase 48 (<=>):** Check <=> before <=  → Int32 (-1/0/1)\n**Phase 50 (===):** Check === before == → Bool (match/no match)\n\n**Common pattern:**\n1. Multi-character operator where substring is valid operator\n2. Lexer: longest match first\n3. Parser: standard precedence table\n4. Type inference: simple return type\n5. No special AST node\n\n## Applicability\nUse for multi-character operators that:\n- Contain valid shorter operators as substrings\n- Are standard comparison/binary operators\n- Don''t need special parsing logic\n\n**Examples:**\n- === before == (this phase)\n- <=> before <= (Phase 48)\n- &. before && (Phase 47)\n- !== before != (if added)\n\n## Future Work\n- Full case/when integration with === semantics\n- Custom === implementations for user types\n- Pattern matching with ===\n- Regex === String matching\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- Checking == before === in lexer: mis-tokenizes ''==='' as ''=='' + ''=''\n- Creating special AST node: unnecessary, Binary handles all cases\n- Different precedence from ==: confusing, should be same level\n\n## Tags\ncrystal, parser, case-equality, triple-equals, comparison-operator, pattern-matching, phase-50, production-ready\n\n## Related\n- Phase 48: Spaceship operator (4275430c)\n- Phase 47: Safe navigation (b8e9fd1b)\n\n## Confidence\n0.95 (fully verified, follows established pattern)','\n',char(10)),replace('# Pattern: === case equality operator implementation\n\n## Solution\nStandard binary operator with precedence 7 (comparison level). Critical: Check === BEFORE == in lexer to avoid mis-tokenization. Returns Bool type for case matching. Reuses existing Binary AST node.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 576 tests pass (564 + 12 new). Commit 4695b5dde.\n\n- Lexer: Added EqEqEq token, check === before == (token.cr:89, lexer...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T22:12:18.251135','2025-10-25T22:12:18.251138',NULL,14,'2025-10-27T20:05:44.043864');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('af6a60c7','general','Phase 51: ||= and &&= Logical Compound Assignment',replace('# Pattern: Logical compound assignment operators ||= and &&=\n\n## Solution\nDirect extension of Phase 20 arithmetic compound assignments. Add OrOrEq and AndAndEq tokens, check in lexer before || and &&, desugar to binary expressions using existing infrastructure.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 588 tests pass (576 + 12 new). Commit c8d22584a.\n\n- Lexer: Added OrOrEq/AndAndEq tokens, check ||= before ||, &&= after &. but before && (token.cr:138-139, lexer.cr:484-515)\n- Parser: Added to assignment check and operator mapping (parser.cr:137-138, 191-192)\n- Desugaring: `a ||= b` => `a = a || b`, `x &&= y` => `x = x && y`\n\n## Key Implementation\n\n**Lexer check order critical:**\n```crystal\nwhen ''&''\n  if next == ''.''   # &. (Phase 47)\n  elsif next == ''&''\n    advance\n    if next == ''=''  # &&= (Phase 51)\n      advance\n      AndAndEq\n    else           # &&\n      AndAnd\n    end\n  else             # & (bitwise)\n    Amp\n  end\n\nwhen ''|''\n  if next == ''|''\n    advance\n    if next == ''=''  # ||= (Phase 51)\n      advance\n      OrOrEq\n    else           # ||\n      OrOr\n    end\n  else             # | (bitwise)\n    Pipe\n  end\n```\n\n**Parser desugaring (same as Phase 20):**\n```crystal\noperator = case assign_token.kind\nwhen Token::Kind::PlusEq     then "+"\nwhen Token::Kind::OrOrEq     then "||"  # Phase 51\nwhen Token::Kind::AndAndEq   then "&&"  # Phase 51\nend\n\n# Create: target = target op rhs\nbinary = Binary(operator, left: target, right: rhs)\nassign = Assign(target, binary)\n```\n\n## Pattern: Phase 20 Extension\n\n**Phase 20 (arithmetic):** `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, `%=`, `**=`\n**Phase 51 (logical):** `||=`, `&&=`\n\n**Same pattern:**\n1. Add token kinds\n2. Add lexer checks (longest match first)\n3. Add to parser assignment check\n4. Add to operator mapping\n5. Desugar to binary expression\n\n**Key difference:**\n- Arithmetic: always evaluates RHS\n- Logical: short-circuit (RHS only if needed)\n- But desugaring identical - short-circuit handled by || and &&\n\n## Semantic Meaning\n\n```crystal\n# Lazy initialization (||=)\n@cache ||= expensive_operation()\n# Only calls expensive_operation() if @cache is nil/false\n\n# Conditional assignment (&&=)\nflag &&= check_condition()\n# Only calls check_condition() if flag is truthy\n```\n\n**Common use cases:**\n- `||=` for memoization/default values\n- `&&=` for conditional updates\n\n## Applicability\nUse Phase 20 pattern for any compound assignment:\n- Bitwise: `&=`, `|=`, `^=`\n- Shift: `<<=`, `>>=`\n- Any binary operator: `op=` => `target = target op rhs`\n\n**Requirements:**\n1. Operator already exists\n2. Multi-character: check longest match first in lexer\n3. Add to assignment check in parser\n4. Add to operator mapping\n\n## Future Extensions\n\nFollowing Phase 20/51 pattern:\n- `&=` (bitwise AND assign)\n- `|=` (bitwise OR assign)\n- `^=` (bitwise XOR assign)\n- `<<=` (left shift assign)\n- `>>=` (right shift assign)\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- Checking || before ||= in lexer: mis-tokenizes ''||='' as ''||'' + ''=''\n- Checking && before &&= in lexer: mis-tokenizes ''&&='' as ''&&'' + ''=''\n- Special-casing logical operators: they follow same desugaring as arithmetic\n- Forgetting &. check before &&: would break safe navigation\n\n## Tags\ncrystal, parser, logical-compound-assignment, or-equals, and-equals, phase-51, production-ready\n\n## Related\n- Phase 20: Arithmetic compound assignments\n- Phase 47: Safe navigation &.\n\n## Confidence\n0.95 (fully verified, direct extension of Phase 20)','\n',char(10)),replace('# Pattern: Logical compound assignment operators ||= and &&=\n\n## Solution\nDirect extension of Phase 20 arithmetic compound assignments. Add OrOrEq and AndAndEq tokens, check in lexer before || and &&, desugar to binary expressions using existing infrastructure.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 588 tests pass (576 + 12 new). Commit c8d22584a.\n\n- Lexer: Added OrOrEq/AndAndEq tokens, check ||= before ||, &&= after &...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T22:31:57.633581','2025-10-25T22:31:57.633583',NULL,16,'2025-10-28T07:31:51.681012');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('feaaafe4','general','Phase 52: Bitwise Compound Assignments (&=, |=, ^=, <<=, >>=)',replace('# Pattern: Bitwise compound assignment operators &=, |=, ^=, <<=, >>=\n\n## Solution\nTrivial extension of Phase 20/51 compound assignment pattern. Add 5 tokens (AmpEq, PipeEq, CaretEq, LShiftEq, RShiftEq), update lexer with critical check order, extend parser assignment check and operator mapping. Desugar to binary expressions: target op= rhs → target = target op rhs.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 603 tests pass (588 + 15 new). Commit 3a6682be1.\n\n- Tokens: Added 5 bitwise compound types (token.cr:140-144)\n- Lexer: Critical check order (lexer.cr):\n  - &: &. → &&= → && → &= → &\n  - |: ||= → || → |= → |\n  - ^: ^= → ^\n  - <: <<= → << → <=> → <= → <\n  - >: >>= → >> → >= → >\n- Parser: Added to assignment check and operator mapping (parser.cr:139-143, 199-203)\n- Tests: 15 comprehensive tests including disambiguation (spec/parser_bitwise_compound_spec.cr)\n\n## Key Implementation\n\n**Lexer check order critical (longest match first):**\n```crystal\nwhen ''&''\n  if next == ''.''\n    AmpDot  # Phase 47: &.\n  elsif next == ''&''\n    advance\n    if next == ''=''\n      advance\n      AndAndEq  # Phase 51: &&=\n    else\n      AndAnd  # &&\n    end\n  elsif next == ''=''\n    advance\n    AmpEq  # Phase 52: &=\n  else\n    Amp  # Phase 21: &\n  end\n```\n\n**Parser desugaring (same as Phase 20/51):**\n```crystal\noperator = case assign_token.kind\nwhen Token::Kind::PlusEq     then "+"\nwhen Token::Kind::OrOrEq     then "||"  # Phase 51\nwhen Token::Kind::AmpEq      then "&"   # Phase 52\nwhen Token::Kind::PipeEq     then "|"   # Phase 52\nwhen Token::Kind::CaretEq    then "^"   # Phase 52\nwhen Token::Kind::LShiftEq   then "<<"  # Phase 52\nwhen Token::Kind::RShiftEq   then ">>"  # Phase 52\nend\n\n# Create: target = target op rhs\nbinary = Binary(operator, left: target, right: rhs)\nassign = Assign(target, binary)\n```\n\n## Pattern Lineage\n\n**Phase 20 (arithmetic):** `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, `%=`, `**=`\n**Phase 51 (logical):** `||=`, `&&=`\n**Phase 52 (bitwise):** `&=`, `|=`, `^=`, `<<=`, `>>=`\n\n**Complete compound assignment coverage achieved.**\n\n## Disambiguation Tests\n\nCritical to verify correct tokenization:\n- `&=` vs `&&=` vs `&&` vs `&`\n- `|=` vs `||=` vs `||` vs `|`\n- `<<=` vs `<<` vs `<`\n- `>>=` vs `>>` vs `>`\n\nAll correctly distinguished via check order.\n\n## Applicability\nCompletes compound assignment family. Pattern applies to any compound assignment operator:\n1. Add token kind\n2. Add lexer check (longest match first if multi-char)\n3. Add to parser assignment check\n4. Add to operator mapping\n5. Desugar to binary expression\n\n**Requirements:**\n- Base operator already exists\n- Multi-character: check longest match first\n- Infrastructure: Phase 20 desugaring mechanism\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- Using hex literals in tests: lexer doesn''t support 0xFF, use decimal 255\n- Wrong check order: checking & before &= breaks &= tokenization\n- Wrong check order: checking << before <<= breaks <<= tokenization\n- Wrong check order: checking | before |= breaks |= tokenization\n- Creating new desugaring: reuse Phase 20 infrastructure\n\n## Future Work\n- Hexadecimal literals (0xFF, 0x0F) - currently unsupported\n- Binary literals (0b1010)\n- Octal literals (0o755)\n\n## Tags\ncrystal, parser, bitwise-compound-assignment, phase-52, production-ready, operators, desugaring\n\n## Related\n- Phase 20: Arithmetic compound assignments\n- Phase 51: Logical compound assignments (af6a60c7)\n- Phase 21: Bitwise operators (&, |, ^, ~)\n- Phase 9: Left shift (<<)\n- Phase 22: Right shift (>>)\n\n## Confidence\n0.98 (fully verified, trivial extension of Phase 20/51)','\n',char(10)),replace('# Pattern: Bitwise compound assignment operators &=, |=, ^=, <<=, >>=\n\n## Solution\nTrivial extension of Phase 20/51 compound assignment pattern. Add 5 tokens (AmpEq, PipeEq, CaretEq, LShiftEq, RShiftEq), update lexer with critical check order, extend parser assignment check and operator mapping. Desugar to binary expressions: target op= rhs → target = target op rhs.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 603 tests pass...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T23:35:34.753892','2025-10-25T23:35:34.753895',NULL,15,'2025-10-27T12:09:00.781882');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('54e4bcba','general','Phase 53: Hexadecimal, Binary, Octal Number Literals',replace('# Pattern: Alternative number literal formats (hex, binary, octal)\n\n## Solution\nExtend lexer to detect prefix (0x, 0b, 0o) and delegate to specialized parsers. No AST changes needed - NumberKind represents TYPE (I32/I64/F64) not FORMAT. Format preserved in token literal slice.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 620 tests pass (603 + 17 new). Commit 042cfacaa.\n\n- Prefix detection: Check 0x/0b/0o in lex_number (lexer.cr:242-253)\n- Three specialized parsers: lex_hex_number, lex_binary_number, lex_octal_number (lexer.cr:312-382)\n- Digit validators: hex_digit?, binary_digit?, octal_digit? (lexer.cr:723-736)\n- Suffix support: Extracted lex_number_suffix helper, reused by all parsers (lexer.cr:384-412)\n- Tests: 17 comprehensive tests covering all formats + contexts (spec/lexer_number_formats_spec.cr)\n\n## Key Implementation\n\n**Prefix detection (case-insensitive):**\n```crystal\nif current_byte == ''0'' && @offset + 1 < @rope.size\n  next_byte = @rope.bytes[@offset + 1]\n  case next_byte\n  when ''x'', ''X'' then return lex_hex_number(...)\n  when ''b'', ''B'' then return lex_binary_number(...)\n  when ''o'', ''O'' then return lex_octal_number(...)\n  end\nend\n# Fall through to decimal parsing\n```\n\n**Specialized parser pattern (example: hex):**\n```crystal\nprivate def lex_hex_number(...)\n  advance  # Skip ''0''\n  advance  # Skip ''x''\n  \n  # Read hex digits\n  while @offset < @rope.size && hex_digit?(current_byte)\n    advance\n  end\n  \n  # Check for suffix\n  number_kind = lex_number_suffix\n  number_kind ||= NumberKind::I32  # Default\n  \n  Token.new(Token::Kind::Number, ...)\nend\n```\n\n**Digit validators:**\n```crystal\nprivate def hex_digit?(byte : UInt8) : Bool\n  ascii_number?(byte) || (byte >= ''a''.ord && byte <= ''f''.ord) || (byte >= ''A''.ord && byte <= ''F''.ord)\nend\n\nprivate def binary_digit?(byte : UInt8) : Bool\n  byte == ''0''.ord || byte == ''1''.ord\nend\n\nprivate def octal_digit?(byte : UInt8) : Bool\n  byte >= ''0''.ord && byte <= ''7''.ord\nend\n```\n\n## Pattern: Lexer Extension\n\n**Lexer-only feature:**\n- Parser sees Number token (no changes needed)\n- Type inference sees NumberKind I32/I64/F64 (no changes needed)\n- Format preserved in token.slice for debugging/error messages\n\n**Key insight:** Format is LEXICAL concern, not semantic. `0xFF` and `255` are identical to parser/type system.\n\n## Applicability\nUse for alternative literal formats where:\n- Syntax is prefix-based (0x, 0b, 0o)\n- Semantic value identical to base format\n- Only lexer tokenization differs\n- Parser/type system agnostic to format\n\n**Examples:**\n- Number bases (this phase)\n- Raw strings (r"...") vs normal strings\n- Character literals (''a'') vs string ("a")\n- Format-preserving literals\n\n## Unblocks\n\n**Phase 52 tests:** Can now use realistic hex literals\n- Before: `flags &= 255` (had to use decimal)\n- After: `flags &= 0xFF` (natural bitwise syntax)\n\n## Future Work\n- Underscores in literals: `0xFF_FF`, `0b1111_0000` (readability)\n- Semantic value conversion (lexer just preserves format, doesn''t compute value)\n- Validate digits match base (currently just stops at invalid digit)\n\n## Anti-patterns\n- Adding new NumberKind for each base (format ≠ type)\n- Parsing value in lexer (lexer tokenizes, later stages evaluate)\n- Changing parser/type inference (lexer-only feature)\n- Case-sensitive prefixes (Crystal allows 0X, 0B, 0O)\n\n## Tags\ncrystal, lexer, number-literals, hexadecimal, binary, octal, phase-53, production-ready\n\n## Related\n- Phase 52: Bitwise compound assignments (feaaafe4) - future work mentioned hex literals\n- Number parsing infrastructure (existing decimal/float/suffix support)\n\n## Confidence\n0.95 (fully verified, standard lexer extension pattern)','\n',char(10)),replace('# Pattern: Alternative number literal formats (hex, binary, octal)\n\n## Solution\nExtend lexer to detect prefix (0x, 0b, 0o) and delegate to specialized parsers. No AST changes needed - NumberKind represents TYPE (I32/I64/F64) not FORMAT. Format preserved in token literal slice.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 620 tests pass (603 + 17 new). Commit 042cfacaa.\n\n- Prefix detection: Check 0x/0b/0o in lex_number (lexer...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-25T23:47:51.324006','2025-10-25T23:47:51.324009',NULL,12,'2025-10-27T00:37:44.839670');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('76632b26','general','Phase 54: String Escape Sequences',replace('# Pattern: String escape sequence processing in lexer with performance optimization\012\012## Solution\012Two-path strategy: fast path (no escapes) uses original slice, slow path (has escapes) processes into new buffer. Store processed strings in @processed_strings array for lifetime management.\012\012## Evidence\012All 636 tests pass (620 + 16 new). Commit 414efdfd2.\012\012- Storage: @processed_strings : Array(Bytes) in Lexer (lexer.cr:13)\012- Pre-scan: Detect escapes/interpolation before processing (lexer.cr:424-433)\012- Fast path: No escapes → use original slice directly (lexer.cr:436-448)\012- Slow path: Process escapes with IO::Memory buffer (lexer.cr:450-496)\012- Escapes: \n, \t, \r, \\, \", \0 → actual characters\012- Unknown escapes: Preserved as-is (\x → \x) for forward compatibility\012- Tests: 16 comprehensive tests (spec/lexer_string_escapes_spec.cr)\012\012## Key Implementation\012\012**Pre-scan detection:**\012```crystal\012scan_offset = @offset\012while scan_offset < @rope.size && @rope.bytes[scan_offset] != DOUBLE_QUOTE\012  byte = @rope.bytes[scan_offset]\012  if byte == HASH && ... # interpolation\012    has_interpolation = true\012  elsif byte == ''\\''.ord.to_u8\012    has_escapes = true\012  end\012  scan_offset += 1\012end\012```\012\012**Fast path (zero overhead):**\012```crystal\012if !has_escapes\012  # Original logic - use slice directly\012  return Token.new(kind, @rope.bytes[from...@offset - 1], span)\012end\012```\012\012**Escape processing:**\012```crystal\012buffer = IO::Memory.new\012while @offset < @rope.size && current_byte != DOUBLE_QUOTE\012  if current_byte == ''\\''.ord.to_u8\012    advance  # Skip backslash\012    case current_byte\012    when ''n''.ord.to_u8 then buffer.write_byte ''\n''.ord.to_u8\012    when ''t''.ord.to_u8 then buffer.write_byte ''\t''.ord.to_u8\012    # ... other escapes ...\012    else  # Unknown escape - keep as is\012      buffer.write_byte ''\\''.ord.to_u8\012      buffer.write_byte current_byte\012    end\012  else\012    buffer.write_byte current_byte\012  end\012  advance\012end\012\012processed_bytes = buffer.to_slice\012@processed_strings << processed_bytes  # Store for lifetime\012Token.new(kind, processed_bytes, span)\012```\012\012## Pattern: Two-Path Optimization\012\012**When to use:**\012- Feature adds processing overhead\012- Common case doesn''t need processing (fast path)\012- Special case needs processing (slow path)\012- Pre-scan cost < processing cost\012\012**Benefits:**\012- Zero overhead for common case\012- Processing only when needed\012- Clean separation of concerns\012\012**Examples:**\012- Strings with/without escapes (this phase)\012- Numbers with/without suffixes (could apply)\012- Identifiers with/without Unicode (future)\012\012## Memory Management\012\012**Problem:** Token.slice must outlive lexer.next_token call\012- Can''t use stack buffers (would be deallocated)\012- Can''t modify rope.bytes (source is immutable)\012\012**Solution:** Store in lexer instance array\012- `@processed_strings : Array(Bytes)` persists with Lexer\012- Each processed string added to array\012- Slices remain valid for token lifetime\012- Lifetime: Lexer instance (acceptable for single-pass lexing)\012\012## Design Decisions\012\012**Why pre-scan:**\012- Detect if processing needed upfront\012- Avoid allocating buffer for simple strings\012- Small cost (single pass) vs big savings (zero processing)\012\012**Why IO::Memory:**\012- Dynamic sizing (don''t know final length)\012- Efficient byte-by-byte writes\012- to_slice() converts to Bytes\012\012**Why preserve unknown escapes:**\012- Forward compatibility (future \u{XXXX})\012- Clear user feedback (escape not supported)\012- Safer than silently dropping backslash\012\012## Applicability\012\012Use two-path optimization when:\012- Feature adds processing cost\012- Common case is simple (>80% of inputs)\012- Pre-scan cost << processing cost\012- Processing changes data (can''t use original)\012\012**Examples:**\012- String escapes (this phase) - most strings don''t have escapes\012- Identifier normalization - most don''t need it\012- Number base conversion - most are decimal\012- Comment stripping - most code isn''t comments\012\012## Future Work\012- Unicode escapes: \u{XXXX}, \uXXXX\012- Hex escapes: \xXX\012- Octal escapes: \OOO\012- Escape in string interpolation: "text #{expr} \n more"\012- Raw strings: r"no\nescapes" (skip processing entirely)\012\012## Anti-patterns\012- Always processing (slow for common case)\012- No pre-scan (allocate buffers unnecessarily)\012- Silently dropping unknown escapes (confusing errors)\012- Stack allocation for processed strings (lifetime issues)\012\012## Tags\012crystal, lexer, string-escapes, two-path-optimization, performance, phase-54, production-ready\012\012## Related\012- Phase 53: Number literal formats (54e4bcba) - lexer-only processing pattern\012- Phase 8: String interpolation (existing - escapes interact with #{})\012\012## Confidence\0120.95 (fully verified, standard escape processing with optimization)','\012',char(10)),replace('# Pattern: String escape sequence processing in lexer with performance optimization\n\n## Solution\nTwo-path strategy: fast path (no escapes) uses original slice, slow path (has escapes) processes into new buffer. Store processed strings in @processed_strings array for lifetime management.\n\n## Evidence\nAll 636 tests pass (620 + 16 new). Commit 414efdfd2.\n\n- Storage: @processed_strings : Array(Bytes) ...','\n',char(10)),NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-26T00:01:12.398054','2025-10-26T00:01:12.398057',NULL,8,'2025-10-27T00:37:44.839991');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('b4fafc14','general','Phase 55: Numeric Separators with Suffix Handling','{"pattern": "Numeric separators in lexer with suffix parsing", "solution": "Accept underscores in digit loops, then rewind past trailing underscores before suffix parsing to prevent conflict", "evidence": "Phase 55: All 653 tests pass including 17 new separator tests. Handles 1_000_i64, 3.14_f64, 0xFF_FF_i64 correctly. Implementation: Modified 5 parser loops (decimal int, decimal frac, hex, binary, octal) to accept underscores, added trailing cleanup after each.", "applicability": "When adding character separators to numeric parsing where suffixes start with same character. Applies to any parser with token-following metadata.", "anti_patterns": ["Skipping separators in single pass without cleanup - causes suffix _i64 to be consumed as separator", "Only cleaning up integer part - fails for floats like 3.14_f64", "Not cleaning up ALL number formats - creates inconsistent behavior"], "confidence": 1.0, "related_entries": ["54e4bcba"]}','{"pattern": "Numeric separators in lexer with suffix parsing", "solution": "Accept underscores in digit loops, then rewind past trailing underscores before suffix parsing to prevent conflict", "evidence": "Phase 55: All 653 tests pass including 17 new separator tests. Handles 1_000_i64, 3.14_f64, 0xFF_FF_i64 correctly. Implementation: Modified 5 parser loops (decimal int, decimal frac, hex, binary...',NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-26T07:52:59.638536','2025-10-26T07:52:59.638540',NULL,3,'2025-10-26T13:52:19.557715');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('378f43b6','general','Phase 56: Character Literals with Escape Sequences','{"pattern": "Character literal lexing with escape sequences", "solution": "Single-quote delimited literals with escape processing. Reuse string escape infrastructure (@processed_strings array) but simpler: no interpolation, no pre-scan. Support same escapes as strings (\\n, \\t, \\r, \\\\, \\'', \\0).", "evidence": "Phase 56: All 670 tests pass (653 + 17 new). Commit 116e272b2. Implementation: lex_char method (67 lines), Token::Kind::Char, ExpressionNode::Kind::Char, parser case.", "applicability": "When adding character literal support to lexer that already has string literals with escapes. Can reuse escape processing infrastructure with delimiter-specific adjustments (\\'' vs \\\").", "anti_patterns": ["Pre-scanning for escapes in tiny data - overhead exceeds benefit", "Creating new storage mechanism - reuse @processed_strings from Phase 54", "Different escape set than strings - confusing for users", "Not storing processed escapes - Token.slice lifetime issues"], "confidence": 0.95, "related_entries": ["76632b26"]}','{"pattern": "Character literal lexing with escape sequences", "solution": "Single-quote delimited literals with escape processing. Reuse string escape infrastructure (@processed_strings array) but simpler: no interpolation, no pre-scan. Support same escapes as strings (\\n, \\t, \\r, \\\\, \\'', \\0).", "evidence": "Phase 56: All 670 tests pass (653 + 17 new). Commit 116e272b2. Implementation: lex_...',NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-26T08:46:51.303204','2025-10-26T08:46:51.303206',NULL,8,'2025-10-27T00:37:41.588695');
INSERT INTO knowledge_entries VALUES('9aceea1f','general','Phase 57: Regex Literals with Context-Aware Disambiguation','{"pattern": "Regex literal lexing with context-aware slash disambiguation", "solution": "Track @last_token_kind to disambiguate ''/'' as division vs regex start. Regex after operators/keywords/delimiters, division after identifiers/numbers/literals/closing brackets. Lex_regex processes pattern with escape preservation, reads optional flags.", "evidence": "Phase 57: All 687 tests pass (670 + 17 new). Commit 591e5bc60. Implementation: @last_token_kind tracking, can_be_regex? context check, lex_regex with flag support. Tests verify both regex and division contexts.", "applicability": "When implementing ambiguous operator/delimiter that needs context for disambiguation. Applies to any language where token meaning depends on previous context (regex, generics, negative literals).", "anti_patterns": ["Lookahead-only disambiguation - fails for x/2 vs /x/ (both start with identifier/slash)", "Regex-only without division - breaks arithmetic", "Complex parser state - lexer-level context tracking sufficient", "Processing escapes in lexer - preserve for regex engine except \\/"], "confidence": 0.95, "related_entries": ["76632b26", "378f43b6"]}','{"pattern": "Regex literal lexing with context-aware slash disambiguation", "solution": "Track @last_token_kind to disambiguate ''/'' as division vs regex start. Regex after operators/keywords/delimiters, division after identifiers/numbers/literals/closing brackets. Lex_regex processes pattern with escape preservation, reads optional flags.", "evidence": "Phase 57: All 687 tests pass (670 + 17 new)....',NULL,'[]','{}',0.8000000000000000444,'2025-10-26T09:10:03.336745','2025-10-26T09:10:03.336747',NULL,5,'2025-10-27T15:52:10.757292');
